# Getting started, step 2: class structure
FinRepStartStepStructure = Class structure
FinRepStructHelp = Accounts are organised in three levels: <b>type</b> (asset, liability, equity, income, expense), <b>class</b> (a subdivision of the type, such as current assets or cost of sales) and the <b>account</b> itself. Here you decide the classes you will use: rename them in your own words, hide the ones you don't need, and add your own classes under a base class (for example "Motor vehicles" under non-current assets). Your classes always add up into their base class, so the reports and templates keep working. The next steps put each account in a type and a class.
FinRepStructCaution = The country column shows what <b>%s</b> requires, as we understand it. Rules change and your situation may differ: check with your accountant before relying on it for statutory or tax reporting.
FinRepStructNoTypeTitle = Need another type?
FinRepStructNoTypeHelp = The five types can be renamed but not added to: the reports are built on them (assets = liabilities + equity, result = income - expenses), and every accounting framework uses the same five. What people usually mean by a new type is already here:<br>- "Cost of goods sold", "Other income", "Administrative expenses", "Current assets": a <b>class</b>: add your own class below.<br>- A contra account (accumulated depreciation, sales returns): the account's <b>contra</b> flag (Account natures, or the Review step).<br>- An account that is sometimes an asset and sometimes a liability (bank, GST / VAT): an <b>alternate nature</b> (Account natures, or the Review step).<br>- Suspense, clearing, undeposited funds: type <b>Clearing</b>.<br>- Memo, statistical or off-balance accounts: type <b>Excluded</b>.
