# How Financial Reporting works (Getting started)
FinRepXTitle = How Financial Reporting works
FinRepXLink = How it works
FinRepXChartsTitle = Countries number and group their accounts differently
FinRepXChartsHelp = Every country (and many companies) has its own chart of accounts. The same kind of account has a different number, and the same number can mean something completely different: <b>512 is the bank in France but employee benefits in the US chart</b>. Dolibarr's <b>Group of account</b> (pcg_type) doesn't help much: some charts put a type in it (US: ASSETS, REVENUE), some a class code (France and OHADA: IMMO, THIRDPARTY, FINAN), some a class description (Germany: "1: Finanz- und Privatkonten"). These are real accounts from charts shipped with Dolibarr.
FinRepXAccount = Account
FinRepXGroup = Dolibarr group
FinRepXChartsNote = Look along the rows: the same eight kinds of account (capital, a vehicle, customers, suppliers, bank, purchases, wages, sales) in each chart. In France, THIRDPARTY holds both customers (an asset) and suppliers (a liability), so a report can't take the type from it. The England chart shipped with Dolibarr is the French plan translated.
FinRepXDolibarrTitle = What Dolibarr has, and what it doesn't
FinRepXDolibarrHelp = Dolibarr has <b>reporting groups</b> (Accounting > Setup > Personalized groups): you can build a report from groups of accounts and formulas. But it has <b>no reliable account type</b> (the Group of account is free text, as above) and <b>no account class</b> (current or non-current, cost of sales, employee costs…). Without them a balance sheet can't be built for any chart, and every report has to be set up account by account. Its personalised report page also only adds up movements over a period, which is right for an income statement but not for a balance sheet.
FinRepXReportsTitle = Countries present their reports differently, some by law
FinRepXReportsHelp = The income statement follows a different structure in each country. Some countries prescribe the lines and their names by law (France, Germany, the OHADA countries); others leave the layout to the company or its accountant (Australian special purpose reports, US private companies).
FinRepXStatutory = lines set by law
FinRepXNotPrescribed = no prescribed layout
FinRepXReportsCaution = Summaries of our understanding of each country's rules, not reviewed by an accountant. Check with your accountant before relying on a layout for statutory or tax reporting.
FinRepXSolveTitle = How this module solves it
FinRepXSolve1 = <b>Every account gets a type and a class</b>, whatever its number or Dolibarr group. The five types are fixed (asset, liability, equity, income, expense, plus clearing and excluded); the classes are yours to rename, hide or extend (step 2). Rules suggest them, from Dolibarr's own setup, your rules, your country's rules and generic rules, and you decide (step 3).
FinRepXSolve2 = <b>The reports work from types and classes</b>, so they work with any chart: trial balance, balance sheet split into current and non-current, income statement, cash flow, receivables and payables. Nothing is dropped: an account without a type is listed apart, and the balance sheet checks that it balances.
FinRepXSolve3 = <b>Checks keep it right</b>: balances on the wrong side, clearing accounts that should be zero, accounts Dolibarr uses for clearing (step 4).
FinRepXSolve4 = <b>Country templates</b> build your income statement the way your country presents it, with its lines and names, as a Dolibarr personalised report (step 5, Report wizard).
FinRepXSolve5 = <b>Your data stays yours</b>: the chart of accounts and the ledger are never changed; types, classes and rules are kept in this module's own tables, and can be exported and shared.

